  {
    "id": "mastodon:3c34d8b60fd2",
    "kind": "article",
    "source": "mastodon",
    "title": "Flowcat: a native-Rust runtime for real-time voice AI agents (self-hosted, one binary) - programming.dev",
    "url": "https://programming.dev/post/52934133",
    "excerpt": "Sharing a project I help maintain — Flowcat, an Apache-2.0 native-Rust runtime\nfor real-time voice AI agents (phone + WebRTC). It runs the whole voice pipeline\n(STT → LLM → TTS, or a single speech-to-speech model) as one self-contained\nbinary you host yourself — in your own VPC or fully air-gapped. No hosted\ncontrol plane, bring your own keys, and a call’s audio never leaves your infra.\nIt’s a clean-room counterpart to pipecat’s architecture, but native Rust: - each\npipeline stage is its own tokio task behind a bounded mpsc channel (natural\nbackpressure) - the hot audio frame is an Arc<AudioFrame>, so each hop moves a\npointer, not a PCM copy - no GIL, so one process uses all cores and holds a flat\ntail under load (measured: flat ~0.6ms p99 framework overhead from 10 to 2,000\nconcurrent calls on one box) - native SIP/RTP, so a single binary can terminate\nphone calls with no FreeSWITCH The linked writeup has the full Rust design + a\nreproducible benchmark vs pipecat (with the honest caveat that it’s framework\noverhead, not end-to-end voice latency — that’s provider-bound). Repo:\nhttps://github.com/AreevAI/flowcat [https://github.com/AreevAI/flowcat]\nApache-2.0, pre-1.0, built in the open. Disclosure: I help maintain it — happy\nto talk architecture or the SIP stack.",
    "author": "suriyan@programming.dev",
    "publishedAt": "2026-07-03T05:24:37.944Z",
    "score": 5,
    "tags": [
      "rust"
    ],
    "labels": [
      "rust"
    ],
    "mentionsByteveda": false
  },
